(; constants used by Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Basic, declared with their translated types ;)

(; //LEAN:Eq.not_lt ;)
Eq__not_lt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (hab : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (not (Lt a b)).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: **Alias** of `ne_of_gt`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LT.lt.ne' ;)
LT__lt__ne' : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Lt b a)) -> Proof (Neq a b).

(; doc: A function `f` is monotone if `a ≤ b` implies `f a ≤ f b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Monotone ;)
Monotone : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `n.ascFactorial k = n (n + 1) ⋯ (n + k - 1)`. This is closely related to `ascPochhammer`, but much less general. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.ascFactorial ;)
Nat__ascFactorial : (n : Elem Nat) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Nat.brecOn ;)
Nat__brecOn : (motive : (Elem Nat -> Set)) -> (t : Elem Nat) -> (F_1 : ((t1 : Elem Nat) -> Elem (Nat__below motive t1) -> motive t1)) -> Elem (motive t).

(; //LEAN:Nat.choose_eq_zero_of_lt._f ;)
Nat__choose_eq_zero_of_lt___f : (x : Elem Nat) -> (f : Elem (Nat__below (x2 => forall Nat (x3 => if (Lt x2 x3) (Eq (Nat__choose x2 x3) 0))) x)) -> (x1 : Elem Nat) -> (h : Proof (Lt x x1)) -> Proof (Eq (Nat__choose x x1) 0).

(; doc: `n.descFactorial k = n! / (n - k)!` (as seen in `Nat.descFactorial_eq_div`), but implemented recursively to allow for "quick" computation when using `norm_num`. This is closely related to `descPochhammer`, but much less general. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.descFactorial ;)
Nat__descFactorial : (n : Elem Nat) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Nat.eq_div_of_mul_eq_right ;)
Nat__eq_div_of_mul_eq_right : (c : Elem Nat) -> (a : Elem Nat) -> (b : Elem Nat) -> (hc : Proof (Neq c 0)) -> (h : Proof (Eq (times c a) b)) -> Proof (Eq a (div b c)).

(; //LEAN:Nat.factorial_ne_zero ;)
Nat__factorial_ne_zero : (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Neq (factorial n) 0).

(; //LEAN:Nat.lt_succ_self ;)
Nat__lt_succ_self : (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Lt n (succ n)).

(; //LEAN:Nat.multichoose_eq._unary ;)
Nat__multichoose_eq___unary : (_x : Elem (PSigma Nat (n => Nat))) -> Proof (Eq (Nat__multichoose (PSigma__proj0 _x) (PSigma__proj1 _x)) (Nat__choose (minus (plus (PSigma__proj0 _x) (PSigma__proj1 _x)) 1) (PSigma__proj1 _x))).

(; doc: Constructs a fully universe-polymorphic dependent pair. ;)
(; //LEAN:PSigma.mk ;)
PSigma__mk : (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (fst : Elem X) -> (snd : Elem (x fst)) -> Elem (PSigma X x).

(; doc: **Function extensionality.** If two functions return equal results for all possible arguments, then they are equal.  It is called “extensionality” because it provides a way to prove two objects equal based on the properties of the underlying mathematical functions, rather than based on the syntax used to denote them. Function extensionality is a theorem that can be [proved using quotient types](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=quotient-funext). ;)
(; //LEAN:funext ;)
funext : (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (f : ((x4 : Elem X) -> x x4)) -> (g : ((x3 : Elem X) -> x x3)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (x2 => Eqt (x x2) (f x2) (g x2)))) -> Proof (Eqt ((x1 : Elem X) -> x x1) f g).

(; //LEAN:lt_of_not_ge ;)
lt_of_not_ge : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (LinearOrder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (not (Leq b a))) -> Proof (Lt a b).

(; //LEAN:mt ;)
mt : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (if a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (not b)) -> Proof (not a).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

